<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahapadma Nanda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahapadma Nanda was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-dynasty">Nanda dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, who around 345 BCE overthrew the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga">Shishunaga</a> king. Tradition describes him as a man of low birth, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> call him a destroyer of the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> dynasties. Ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, he extended Magadhan power over most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> valley, creating the first large empire of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahapadma-nanda.jpg" alt="Mahapadma Nanda" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Michel Moullec from Vern sur Seiche (35, Bretagne), France · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I12_1karshapana_Maghada_1ar_(8482307176).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>What is told of Mahapadma comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, which make him the first Nanda king and the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahanandin">Mahanandin</a>, the last king of the preceding Shaishunaga line, by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudra</a> woman. His name, also given as Mahapadma-pati, means lord of the great lotus. Other traditions about the founder of the dynasty do not use this name at all.</p><p>Buddhist texts call the first Nanda king Ugrasena and describe him as of unknown lineage. According to the Mahavamsa-tika he came from the frontier region, was captured by a gang of robbers and later became their leader. One theory holds that Ugrasena was probably another name for Mahapadma. Jain texts such as the Parishishtaparvan and the Avashyaka sutra say that the Nanda king was the son of a courtesan by a barber and that he succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udayin">Udayin</a>, with Kalpaka, a Jain, as his chief minister.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> describe him as ekarat, sole sovereign, and as sarva-kshatrantaka, destroyer of all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriyas</a>, the warriors and rulers. The lines he is said to have exterminated include the Maithalas, Kasheyas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikshvaku">Ikshvakus</a>, Panchalas, Shurasenas, Kurus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haihaya">Haihayas</a>, Vitihotras, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalingas</a> and Ashmakas.</p><p>The length of his reign is uncertain. The Matsya Purana assigns him 88 years, the Vayu Purana only 28. Modern estimates of his coronation also differ: the Indologist F. E. Pargiter put it in 382 BCE, the historian R. K. Mukherjee in 364 BCE and the historian H. C. Raychaudhuri about 345 BCE, and the beginning of Nanda rule has been placed as early as the 5th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, eight sons of Mahapadma ruled in succession after him for twelve years in all; only one, Sukalpa, is named. The Buddhist texts instead make the next eight kings brothers of the first, give the dynasty 22 years in total and name the last of them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhana-nanda">Dhana Nanda</a>.</p><p>Greek and Roman writers call the Nanda king of the time of Alexander's invasion Agrammes, possibly a corruption of the Sanskrit Augraseniya, son or descendant of Ugrasena. They suggest that the founder was a barber who took the throne from the last king of the earlier dynasty. The Roman historian Curtius, of the 1st century CE, reports on the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> that this barber became the queen's lover, murdered the king, seized power while pretending to act as guardian of the princes and later killed them, and that the king reigning in Alexander's day was his son.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahapadma Nanda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 345 BCE, although the date is uncertain, <b>Mahapadma Nanda</b> overthrew the last Shishunaga king, whom the Puranas name Mahanandin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 345 BCE <b>Mahapadma Nanda</b>, said by tradition to be of low birth, overthrew the last Shishunaga ruler of the Kingdom of Magadha.</blockquote><a href="/state/nanda-empire">Nanda Empire</a></li></ul></section>
